::CW_U02_L08_UA_Q01::Passage: "The apartment smelled like rain and burnt toast. Nia placed her college envelope beside the sink and waited for Grandma to notice." What is the strongest interpretation of the setting details?{
~A. They only prove breakfast happened.#Incorrect. Burnt toast suggests daily life, but the envelope and waiting create emotional pressure.
=B. They place a major decision inside an ordinary home moment.#Correct. The familiar apartment details contrast with the importance of the college envelope.
~C. They remove conflict from the scene.#Incorrect. Waiting for Grandma to notice creates tension.
~D. They show the narrator is outside.#Incorrect. The apartment and sink place the scene indoors.
}
::CW_U02_L08_UA_Q02::Passage: "You tell yourself the stage lights are too bright to see anyone, but you still find your father's empty seat." What effect does second person create?{
~A. It gives every audience member a speaking role.#Incorrect. The narration focuses on one character's experience.
~B. It turns the scene into a research report.#Incorrect. The passage remains narrative.
~C. It removes emotion from the moment.#Incorrect. The empty seat creates emotion.
=D. It pulls the reader into the character's self-directed pressure.#Correct. "You tell yourself" makes the inner moment immediate and personal.
}
::CW_U02_L08_UA_Q03::Passage: A writer wants to show that a character is becoming more honest. Which action best supports that purpose?{
=A. The character crosses out a false sentence and writes the harder truth beneath it.#Correct. The visible revision shows movement from hiding to honesty.
~B. The character changes the subject whenever asked a question.#Incorrect. That shows avoidance, not growing honesty.
~C. The character describes the weather for a full page.#Incorrect. Weather description must connect to the conflict to support the purpose.
~D. The character hides the page before anyone sees it.#Incorrect. Hiding the truth weakens the honesty arc.
}
::CW_U02_L08_UA_Q04::Passage: "The bike chain slipped again at the bottom of the hill. Omar looked up at the hospital windows and started pushing." What does the action reveal?{
~A. Omar quits because the bike breaks.#Incorrect. He starts pushing, so he continues despite the problem.
~B. Omar is only interested in machines.#Incorrect. The hospital windows suggest a human reason for continuing.
=C. Omar persists because the destination matters.#Correct. The broken bike adds obstacle, and his action shows determination.
~D. Omar has no goal.#Incorrect. Looking at the hospital gives direction and urgency.
}
::CW_U02_L08_UA_Q05::Passage: A draft uses a full page of backstory before the character enters the contest room. The unit skill focus is narrative scene development. What revision best improves pacing?{
=A. Begin closer to the contest room and weave only necessary background into the action.#Correct. This keeps the reader inside the important moment while preserving needed context.
~B. Add another page of unrelated backstory.#Incorrect. More delay will likely weaken the scene.
~C. Remove the contest room and keep only history.#Incorrect. The contest room creates the immediate scene pressure.
~D. Use only vague summary and no concrete moment.#Incorrect. Scene development needs specific action and detail.
}
::CW_U02_L08_UA_Q06::Passage: "I said I did not care who won, but I checked the scoreboard before I checked my ankle." What does the narrator's behavior show?{
~A. The narrator has no conflict.#Incorrect. The contradiction creates inner conflict.
~B. The scoreboard is unrelated.#Incorrect. It reveals what the narrator values in the moment.
~C. The narrator is describing a meal.#Incorrect. The passage concerns a competition or game.
=D. The narrator's actions contradict the claim of not caring.#Correct. Checking the scoreboard first reveals hidden investment.
}
::CW_U02_L08_UA_Q07::Passage: "The garden gate hung open, and the tomatoes lay crushed under one set of muddy footprints." Which technique is strongest?{
~A. Explaining every cause immediately.#Incorrect. The passage leaves readers to infer what happened.
=B. Indirectly creating a problem through setting detail.#Correct. The open gate and footprints imply an event without directly explaining it.
~C. Removing all visual detail.#Incorrect. The sentence depends on visual detail.
~D. Switching to second person.#Incorrect. The passage is not written as "you."
}
::CW_U02_L08_UA_Q08::Passage: Which choice best states how point of view supports narrative meaning?{
~A. Point of view only decides paragraph length.#Incorrect. It shapes access to knowledge, feelings, and reliability.
~B. Point of view has no connection to character.#Incorrect. It strongly affects how readers understand character.
=C. Point of view controls what the reader can know and how close the reader feels to events.#Correct. Narrator position influences information, trust, and emotional distance.
~D. Point of view is always third person.#Incorrect. Narratives can use first, second, or third person.
}
::CW_U02_L08_UA_Q09::Passage: "Mara kept the receipt for the returned dress taped inside her locker." What question would most help revise the detail for meaning?{
~A. What color is every locker in the school?#Incorrect. That may add setting, but it does not connect the receipt to character meaning.
~B. How many lockers are in the hallway?#Incorrect. A count does not deepen the scene's meaning.
~C. What brand made the tape?#Incorrect. Brand detail is unlikely to matter unless connected to the conflict.
=D. Why does Mara keep proof of something she gave back?#Correct. This question asks how the object reveals desire, regret, or conflict.
}
::CW_U02_L08_UA_Q10::Passage: Scene purpose: show a character deciding to speak up. Which ending best completes the arc?{
=A. The character hears the room go quiet and says the first true sentence aloud.#Correct. The action shows the decision under pressure.
~B. The character lowers their hand and lets someone else answer.#Incorrect. That avoids speaking up.
~C. The character leaves before the meeting starts.#Incorrect. Leaving removes the decision moment.
~D. The character thinks about speaking but the scene ends.#Incorrect. The purpose asks for a decision, not only a thought.
}
::CW_U02_L08_UA_Q11::Passage: "The necklace clicked against the microphone as Priya leaned forward." Which integrated effect is strongest if Priya is about to confess?{
~A. The necklace proves Priya is wealthy.#Incorrect. No evidence supports that claim.
~B. The microphone removes the need for conflict.#Incorrect. It can make the conflict more public.
=C. The sound increases attention on the tense public moment.#Correct. The object, sound, and microphone setting all heighten the confession.
~D. The sound has no possible purpose.#Incorrect. Sound can focus mood and tension.
}
::CW_U02_L08_UA_Q12::Passage: "Every time Leo described the fire, the match moved from the sidewalk to his own hand." What should a reader notice?{
~A. The story is becoming more reliable.#Incorrect. Changing details often create doubt.
=B. Leo's account may reveal guilt or unreliable narration.#Correct. The moving match changes responsibility and affects trust.
~C. The setting is a beach.#Incorrect. The passage names sidewalk and fire, not beach.
~D. The sentence has no conflict.#Incorrect. The changing account creates conflict.
}
::CW_U02_L08_UA_Q13::Passage: A student writes, "The room was nice. The person was sad. The thing mattered." What revision would best improve craft?{
=A. Replace vague words with specific details that show the room, person, and object in action.#Correct. Specific, purposeful details help readers infer emotion and meaning.
~B. Keep all vague words because they are easier.#Incorrect. Vague wording weakens scene development.
~C. Remove the character from the scene.#Incorrect. Character is central to narrative.
~D. Add unrelated facts about weather only.#Incorrect. Unrelated details do not solve vague craft.
}
::CW_U02_L08_UA_Q14::Passage: "The train pulled away before Elena reached the platform. She laughed, then covered her mouth with both hands." What does the reaction suggest?{
~A. Elena is definitely happy with no conflict.#Incorrect. The covered mouth complicates the laugh.
~B. Elena is not in a setting.#Incorrect. The train platform is the setting.
~C. Elena controls the train schedule.#Incorrect. No evidence supports that.
=D. Elena feels a complicated response she is trying to control.#Correct. Laughing and covering her mouth suggest mixed emotion or surprise.
}
::CW_U02_L08_UA_Q15::Passage: Which option best defines an integrated narrative scene?{
~A. A scene with the longest possible paragraphs.#Incorrect. Length does not guarantee integration.
~B. A scene with no conflict.#Incorrect. Narrative scenes usually need tension, desire, or change.
=C. A scene where point of view, character, setting, pacing, and details work together toward one purpose.#Correct. Integration means craft choices support a unified effect.
~D. A scene made only of summary.#Incorrect. Scene development uses action, setting, dialogue, and detail.
}
::CW_U02_L08_UA_Q16::Passage: "The room went silent after I said the name. Even the aquarium filter seemed to stop." What does the aquarium detail do?{
~A. It randomly changes the topic to pets.#Incorrect. The detail is connected to silence and tension.
=B. It extends the silence through a concrete sensory detail.#Correct. The filter detail makes the silence feel larger and more noticeable.
~C. It proves the narrator is underwater.#Incorrect. An aquarium in a room does not place the narrator underwater.
~D. It removes the importance of the spoken name.#Incorrect. It emphasizes the effect of the name.
}
::CW_U02_L08_UA_Q17::Passage: A writer needs to revise a scene for standard MLA.CW.FIC.03, which focuses on character and setting. Which revision fits best?{
~A. Delete all setting so only thoughts remain.#Incorrect. The standard requires attention to setting.
~B. Add a random object that never appears again.#Incorrect. A random object may not develop character or setting.
~C. Remove the character's decision.#Incorrect. Character development depends on meaningful choice or response.
=D. Add a setting detail that pressures the character's choice.#Correct. Setting becomes meaningful when it affects character action or understanding.
}
::CW_U02_L08_UA_Q18::Passage: "Third-person limited follows Jade as she waits outside the office." What can the reader usually know?{
=A. Jade's experience closely, with limited access to others.#Correct. Third-person limited stays near one character while using third-person pronouns.
~B. Only facts from every mind in the building.#Incorrect. That describes a more omniscient approach.
~C. Nothing about Jade's feelings.#Incorrect. Limited third person can reveal Jade's thoughts and feelings.
~D. The reader becomes the character through "you."#Incorrect. That is second person.
}
::CW_U02_L08_UA_Q19::Passage: "The empty chair at the end of the table stayed pulled out all dinner." What does the detail most likely suggest?{
~A. The table is too small for plates.#Incorrect. The passage focuses on absence, not table size.
=B. A missing person or unresolved absence affects the meal.#Correct. The pulled-out empty chair draws attention to someone not there.
~C. The chair has no meaning.#Incorrect. Its placement and emphasis make it meaningful.
~D. Everyone ignored the chair successfully.#Incorrect. The sentence itself keeps attention on it.
}
::CW_U02_L08_UA_Q20::Passage: A draft has the line, "I was nervous." Which revision best shows rather than only tells?{
~A. I was very, very nervous.#Incorrect. This intensifies telling but does not show behavior.
~B. Nervous means worried.#Incorrect. Defining the word does not create a scene.
=C. I counted the same three floor tiles until my name was called.#Correct. The repeated counting shows nervousness through action.
~D. The room was a room.#Incorrect. This adds no meaningful detail.
}
::CW_U02_L08_UA_Q21::Passage: "The narrator refuses to explain why she hides the medal, but she checks the drawer every night." What is the best reader inference?{
=A. The medal carries unresolved emotional importance.#Correct. Hiding and checking it repeatedly show it matters even without full explanation.
~B. The medal is definitely worthless.#Incorrect. Her repeated checking suggests value.
~C. The drawer is the main character.#Incorrect. The drawer is part of the action, not the character.
~D. The narrator has forgotten the medal.#Incorrect. She checks it nightly.
}
::CW_U02_L08_UA_Q22::Passage: Which feedback best helps a student whose scene has many details but no clear purpose?{
~A. Add more unrelated details.#Incorrect. More unrelated detail increases confusion.
~B. Remove all concrete detail.#Incorrect. The goal is purposeful detail, not no detail.
=C. Choose the scene's main change, then keep or revise details that support that change.#Correct. Purpose guides which details belong and how they should function.
~D. Change every sentence to a question.#Incorrect. Sentence form alone will not solve purpose.
}
::CW_U02_L08_UA_Q23::Passage: "When the principal asked who painted the mural, Sam looked at the blue stain on his sleeve." What does the stain do?{
~A. It proves Sam is unrelated to the mural.#Incorrect. It suggests the opposite.
~B. It changes the narration to first person.#Incorrect. The sentence remains third person.
~C. It makes the scene about laundry only.#Incorrect. Laundry is not the central conflict.
=D. It reveals evidence through a small character detail.#Correct. The stain connects Sam physically to the mural.
}
::CW_U02_L08_UA_Q24::Passage: "The doorbell rang once. Nobody moved. It rang again, and Aunt Celia set down the carving knife." What pacing technique is used?{
~A. Summary skips the moment entirely.#Incorrect. The passage slows the moment.
=B. Repetition and pause build suspense before action.#Correct. The repeated ring and delayed movement stretch tension.
~C. A flashback explains childhood.#Incorrect. No flashback appears.
~D. The scene becomes informational writing.#Incorrect. It remains narrative.
}
::CW_U02_L08_UA_Q25::Passage: Which final revision question best prepares a scene for unit mastery?{
=A. Do my point of view, character choices, setting details, pacing, and key objects all support the same intended effect?#Correct. This checks the major Unit 2 craft skills together.
~B. Did I use the most words possible?#Incorrect. More words do not automatically improve craft.
~C. Did I remove every concrete detail?#Incorrect. Concrete details are essential when purposeful.
~D. Did I avoid revising the scene?#Incorrect. Revision is part of mastery.
}
